### What is the problem with in-place (blocking) retries?

The classic in-place retry done by DefaultErrorHandler is blocking: while it retries a record with backoff, the consumer thread for that partition is held, so every record behind it waits. For long backoffs or many retries this can stall the entire partition for minutes.

```text
Blocking retry (DefaultErrorHandler):
Record A fails -> retry in 1s -> retry in 2s -> retry in 4s
During those 7 seconds, records B, C, D are WAITING
Partition throughput: zero for 7 seconds
```

### How does @RetryableTopic make retries non-blocking?

@RetryableTopic moves the failed record off the main topic onto a dedicated retry topic and lets the main consumer continue immediately. A separate consumer reads the retry topic after the backoff delay.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.RetryableTopic;
import org.springframework.kafka.retrytopic.DltStrategy;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class NonBlockingRetryListener {

    @RetryableTopic(
        attempts = "4",
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 2.0),
        dltStrategy = DltStrategy.FAIL_ON_ERROR
    )
    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(String event) {
        System.out.println("Processing: " + event);
        // If this throws, record moves to retry topic, main consumer continues
        settlementService.process(event);
    }

    @org.springframework.kafka.annotation.DltHandler
    public void handleDlt(String event) {
        System.out.println("DLT: " + event + " -- alerting ops team");
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: {"account":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
# If it fails:
# -> settlements-retry-0 (after 1s)
# -> settlements-retry-1 (after 2s)
# -> settlements-retry-2 (after 4s)
# -> settlements-dlt (terminal)
```

### What topics does @RetryableTopic create?

Spring auto-creates retry topics and the DLT based on the original topic name and the number of attempts.

```text
Original topic:     settlements
Retry topic 0:      settlements-retry-0     (delay: 1 second)
Retry topic 1:      settlements-retry-1     (delay: 2 seconds)
Retry topic 2:      settlements-retry-2     (delay: 4 seconds)
Dead letter topic:  settlements-dlt         (terminal)

# Each retry topic has its own consumer that waits the delay before reading
```

### How is a retry topic different from the DLT?

A retry topic is a transient stage where records are expected to succeed on a later attempt once a transient problem clears. The DLT is the end of the line: records there have failed every attempt and will not be retried automatically.

| Aspect | Retry Topic | Dead Letter Topic |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Temporary holding for reprocessing | Terminal parking for failed records |
| Records reprocessed? | Yes, after delay | No (unless manual replay) |
| Expected outcome | Success after transient fix | Human inspection / alerting |
| Consumer | Automatic, reads after delay | Optional @DltHandler or separate consumer |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class RetryVsDltDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Retry topic: record will be tried again after delay");
        System.out.println("DLT:         record will NOT be tried again automatically");
        System.out.println("Retry topic: transient failure recovery");
        System.out.println("DLT:         permanent failure parking");
    }
}
```

```text
Retry topic: record will be tried again after delay
DLT:         record will NOT be tried again automatically
Retry topic: transient failure recovery
DLT:         permanent failure parking
```

### How do you prevent infinite retry loops?

Two controls prevent infinite loops. First, bounded attempts: the `attempts` value is a hard cap. Second, exception classification: permanent failures skip retries entirely.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.RetryableTopic;
import org.springframework.retry.annotation.Backoff;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class ClassifiedRetryListener {

    @RetryableTopic(
        attempts = "4",
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 1000, multiplier = 2.0),
        exclude = { javax.validation.ValidationException.class } // skip retries for validation errors
    )
    @KafkaListener(topics = "settlements", groupId = "settlement-group")
    public void onSettlement(String event) {
        validate(event);  // ValidationException -> DLT immediately
        process(event);   // DB timeout -> retry through stages
    }

    void validate(String event) { /* ... */ }
    void process(String event) { /* ... */ }
}
```

```text
# Transient error (DB timeout): retry-0 -> retry-1 -> retry-2 -> DLT
# Permanent error (invalid account): skip retries -> DLT immediately
```

### How does @DltHandler process dead letter records?

You can annotate a method with `@DltHandler` to process whatever lands in the DLT, for example to persist it for triage or send an alert.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.DltHandler;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DltProcessor {

    @DltHandler
    public void handleDlt(String event) {
        System.out.println("DLT received: " + event);
        // Save to a triage table for ops team
        // Send Slack alert
        // Log for audit trail
    }
}
```

```text
DLT received: {"account":"SAV2001","amount":50000}
```

### What are the trade-offs of non-blocking retries?

Non-blocking retries change ordering because a retried record is reprocessed later than records that came after it on the main topic. Use this pattern only where strict per-key ordering is not required. Each retry topic needs partitions and consumers, adding operational overhead.

```text
# Ordering impact:
# Main topic: [A, B, C]  (A fails)
# A moves to retry topic
# B and C process normally on main topic
# A is reprocessed from retry topic AFTER B and C

# If A and B are for the same account and order matters: use blocking retries
# If order does not matter (e.g., independent events): use non-blocking retries
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Keep processing idempotent because a record can be delivered more than once across retry stages. Blocking retries via DefaultErrorHandler remain the better choice when strict per-key ordering must be preserved and recovery is expected to be fast. Each retry topic adds operational overhead (partitions, consumers, monitoring), so size the retry chain appropriately.

```bash
# Monitor retry topics and DLT
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group settlement-group \
  --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092

# Expected: retry topics should have low lag (records move through quickly)
# DLT should have near-zero lag (triaged regularly)
```
